* with (g v = 1 / sqrt (1 - v ^ 2 / c ^ 2))
	* forward
		* x' = (g v) * (x  - v * t )
		* t' = (g v) * (t  + (v / c ^ 2) * x )
	* backward [the negativity-change of v]
		* x  = (g v) * (x' + v * t')
		* t  = (g v) * (t' - (v / c ^ 2) * x')
* can be derived from elementary space properties like homogeneity, isotropy, ... and relativity
	* one good source is
		* Nianjun Tan, Hangzhou, China: A Symmetry-First Elementary Derivation of the Lorentz Transformation
			\ links-to external https://arxiv.org/html/2605.25159#Sx3.E1
			* additional notes to help understanding
				* the 'f' coefficient function outputs are not all scalar-dimensioned; for example the dimension of f14 output is velocity
				* no need for trigonometry [the tan function], instead just assume (v = u); this trick also works in finding the boost-invariant speeds
	* additional product of the derivation is
		* the velocity composition
			* (u + v)  / (1 - u * v / R)
* the consistency of this transform can be verified by proving ["checking"] its group properties (invertability, associativity)
